source: A short test of slides, split screens and the shared screen. Not a lesson.
---
title: The stage
say: This is the board, as before.
write a: y = f(g(x))
prepare: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_rule
prepare: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Derivative
say: Now the stage changes to slides.
slides: test/stage
show: slides
say: The lecturer can mark a named part of a slide.
mark: highlight slide.inside
say: Here is the next slide.
slide: next
say: It can mark an equation on a slide, and write in an empty area.
mark: box slide.rule
write w @work: \frac{dy}{dx} = 5 \cdot 3 = 15
say: The board can go on the left, with the slides on the right.
show: board | slides
say: Anything written now goes on the board, next to the slide.
write b: g'(x) = 15(3x+1)^4
say: Two slides can be shown at once, with the board beside them.
show: slides 1 | slides 2 | board
say: Now a real web page, on a shared screen. Two tabs were opened before they were needed.
show: screen
tab: 1
say: It can be scrolled while the lecturer talks.
scroll: 500
say: Here is the other tab.
tab: 2
say: And the web page can sit beside the board, with the slides underneath.
show: screen | board / slides
say: Then back to the board alone.
show: board
say: That is the end of the test.
